Date: 1 Aug 2026, 06:13
From: Mathew Wedel <mathew.wedel@gmail.com>
To: Mike Taylor <sauropoda@gmail.com>
Subject: Re: The Barosaurus Mount manuscript is done!

Congratulations!

I don't know if this will be welcome or not, but after having the manuscript percolate on my wetware for two nights and a day, I have a few more suggestions. All pretty minor, all optional, none requiring very much work. They're down in the postscript.

Best,

Matt

P.S. Further suggestions, to take or leave:

Do you say anywhere in the paper that the presacrals are on display? You can editorialize (or not) about their visibility, but at least a sentence somewhere on the fact that they’re out for the public to see would be useful, at least insofar as the paper seems incomplete without it (and apologies if you already have this in and I missed it).
 
Another thing you could include or not – it’s wild that the rearing Baro was the first big job for RCI. At some point when you talk about it being the tallest free-standing mounted dinosaur skeleton in the world, you might mention that RCI also mounted the Berlin Giraffatitan, which is probably the second-tallest mount (I’m not sure if counts as free-standing since it has support poles, but still, it seemed to me an interesting point).

A few quick thoughts on the Discussion.

1. The final section, Size of the AMNH 6341, really only deals with neck length and height. If there are published mass estimates (the only obvious ones I can think of would be by Don Henderson and maaaaybe Greg Paul), I think it would be good to include them, especially if they have any interesting differences from mass estimates for Diplodocus by the same authors. I know you’re not trying to make this the be all, end all paper on Barosaurus generally, just on this specimen, but a section on size that only hits linear measurements seems incomplete.

2. I think the discussion would read better in a different order. As it structured right now, it goes Rearing Pose -> Short History of Rearing Sauropods -> Cultural Impact -> Size of the AMNH 6341 Animal. I’d put the size bit up front, before Rearing Pose, to improve the flow. As it is, it goes progressively broader in the first three sections, then swerves back to (what will be for many readers) a very narrow scientific question. Plus, IMHO Rearing Pose will hit different coming right on the heels of the discussion of the height of the mount.

3. I don’t know why I didn’t comment on this the first time around – I remember thinking that it was pretty jarring to go from the paragraph on the height of the mount straight into Acknowledgements. You may think it’s stupid, but I think at least a short Conclusion would be useful as an outro. Instead of doing the standard “here’s a summary of everything you just read”, you might use that to punch up the most novel (and therefore surprising) parts of the story. Things that come to mind: (1) the irony of Barnum Brown doing all the wheeling and dealing to assemble that magnificent specimen only for nothing to be done with it for decades, (2) the value of paleoart as a hypothesis-generating machine – there would be no rearing Baro without Ambush at Como, (3) Dingus and Gaffney independently having the idea for a rearing mount, and all the decision-makers being surprised that the others, including Norell, were up for it, (4) the belated validation of the biomechanical feasibility of rearing sauropods by Heinrich Mallison. And maybe a good point to end the paper on would be that the mount achieved its stated goals of provoking not just public discussion, but much further scientific work – I’m thinking here not just about Heinrich’s work, but also all the papers on sauropod blood pressure that the mount inspired. (If you already have something like that in, just rearrange the Discussion to put it at the tail end.)


